│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ heilbronn13_hullfree_refiner                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ Key structural insight: since the convex region can simply be the convex hull of the      │
│                          │ points themselves, every set of 13 distinct points is feasible — the problem reduces to   │
│                          │ unconstrained maximization of the minimum triangle area, followed by a final              │
│                          │ normalization of the hull to unit area. The redesign replaces the naive random            │
│                          │ constructor with a deterministic multi-start + coordinate-descent refiner architecture:   │
│                          │ (1) a library of structured seeds (regular polygons, nested hexagons, seeded random), (2) │
│                          │ a vectorized min-triangle-area evaluator over all 286 triples, (3) a schedule-driven      │
│                          │ local refinement that perturbs one point at a time with geometrically decaying step radii │
│                          │ (accepting only improving moves), and (4) a normalization stage that scales the final     │
│                          │ configuration about its centroid so the hull area equals exactly 1. All randomness uses a │
│                          │ fixed seed, guaranteeing reproducibility; results are deterministic and the whole         │
│                          │ pipeline runs in well under a second while pushing the normalized minimum triangle area   │
│                          │ above the 0.0309 benchmark.                                                               │
