│ patch_description        │ Instead of returning random points, use a two-phase approach: (1) initialize with a       │
│                          │ 3-fold rotationally symmetric configuration (regular 12-gon plus center, adapted to n=13) │
│                          │ which is known to be a strong candidate for Heilbronn-type problems, and (2) perform a    │
│                          │ deterministic projected local search — iteratively perturb points along gradient          │
│                          │ directions that increase the minimum triangle area (using the smallest triangle's         │
│                          │ vertices to guide movement), with shrinking step sizes and convex-hull normalization. The │
│                          │ final configuration is scaled so the convex hull has exactly unit area, maximizing        │
│                          │ min_area_normalized. This exploits the symmetry heuristics and scale invariance described │
│                          │ in the problem context, and should dramatically outperform the random baseline.           │
