│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ targeted_argmin_anneal13                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program plateaued because hill-climbing on the global minimum is flat: most  │
│                          │ random moves don't change the min at all, so nothing is accepted. Following the           │
│                          │ recommendation, I rewrite the search to (1) target the argmin triangle directly — perturb │
│                          │ only points that participate in one of the k smallest triangles, and use a *targeted      │
│                          │ geometric move* that pushes a vertex perpendicular away from the line through the other   │
│                          │ two vertices of its tiny triangle (the exact direction that increases that triangle's     │
│                          │ area), and (2) use a smooth acceptance score (mean of the k smallest triangle areas) so   │
│                          │ the optimizer receives gradient-like pressure on the whole "small triangle" set while the │
│                          │ true global min is tracked separately as the gate for the best-so-far. Multiple           │
│                          │ deterministic initializations (regular 12-gon + center, radius-alternating ring + center, │
│                          │ triangle-symmetric lattice, perturbed grid) are each annealed, then the overall best is   │
│                          │ polished by strict min-increasing local moves and rescaled so the convex hull has unit    │
│                          │ area. All area computations are fully vectorized over the 286 triples, keeping runtime    │
│                          │ ~1–2 s.                                                                                   │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0225                                                                                   │
