│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ k_smallest_softmin_hillclimb                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current hill climbing only accepts moves that strictly increase the single global     │
│                          │ minimum area. This creates huge flat plateaus: moving one vertex of the argmin triangle   │
│                          │ usually doesn't help (another triangle becomes the min), so the search stalls at          │
│                          │ min_area_normalized ≈ 0.02. Following the recommendation, I modify `_polish` to (a) rank  │
│                          │ moves by a soft objective — the mean of the k smallest triangle areas — which gives       │
│                          │ targeted pressure to lift the whole bottom tail, and (b) re-check the true global minimum │
│                          │ as the gate for committing a new best configuration. This escapes local traps where       │
│                          │ improving the bottom-k average is possible but the strict min stays flat, and then        │
│                          │ periodically locks in true min improvements. I also apply the same soft pressure inside   │
│                          │ `_anneal` by widening k_bot and biasing vertex selection toward the very worst triangles. │
