│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ smooth_score_adaptive_anneal                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current program regressed vs. the 0.77-scoring ancestor because (a) each Metropolis   │
│                          │ step recomputes the full hull area (O(n log n) Python) inside `_score`, making annealing  │
│                          │ both slow and noisy, and (b) annealing directly on the hard minimum gets stuck in flat    │
│                          │ minima. The top prior program used a *smooth* objective (mean of the K smallest triangle  │
│                          │ areas, unnormalized during optimization, since the problem is scale-invariant and the     │
│                          │ hull barely changes during local moves) with targeted geometric moves, which is a         │
│                          │ strictly better search landscape. I restore that smooth-score annealing with targeted     │
│                          │ "push away from the opposite edge" moves, and — per the recommendation — replace the      │
│                          │ fixed ×0.88/600 step ladder with **adaptive step control**: track the acceptance rate     │
│                          │ over the last 200 proposals; if below 15% halve the step, if above 50% multiply by 1.5    │
│                          │ (capped at 0.05). This keeps the anneal in the productive acceptance band longer. The     │
│                          │ final selection is done on the true normalized minimum (area/hull area), so the smooth    │
│                          │ surrogate only steers the search, not the reported answer. I also trim polish iterations  │
│                          │ to keep eval time near the 5s regime.                                                     │
