╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ targeted_vertex_relaxation                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ Restructured around a "worst-triangle feedback loop" architecture: each iteration         │
│                          │ computes all 286 triangle areas once (vectorized cross products, cheaper and more stable  │
│                          │ than Heron), identifies the k smallest triangles, and only proposes moves to vertices of  │
│                          │ those worst triangles — pushing a vertex away from its opposite edge, which is the direct │
│                          │ geometric gradient direction for increasing a degenerate triangle's area. Data flow is    │
│                          │ organized as: seed bank → coarse ring-annealing → targeted worst-triangle relaxation →    │
│                          │ fine greedy polish → hull-normalization. Moves that shrink hull area also help the ratio, │
│                          │ so hull area is recomputed incrementally only when a hull vertex moves. This focuses all  │
│                          │ compute on the binding constraints (the minimal triangles) instead of wasting evaluations │
│                          │ on random vertices, improving min_area_normalized at similar runtime.                     │
