│ patch_name               │ revert_to_vectorized_ring_anneal                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The current program regressed significantly (0.57 vs 0.77 for the prior best). Two key    │
│                          │ problems: (1) `_anneal` and `_polish` now call `_score`/`_soft_score` which recompute the │
│                          │ *convex hull* (a slow Python pure loop) on every candidate evaluation — this dominates    │
│                          │ the ~10s runtime while delivering worse results; (2) the seeds are confined to [0,1] with │
│                          │ clipping to the square boundary, and the vertex targeting samples from a pooled set of    │
│                          │ bottom-k vertices rather than picking a vertex of a *specific* worst triangle, weakening  │
│                          │ the geometric "push away from the opposite edge" move. The best prior version (score      │
│                          │ 0.77) used vectorized area scoring only (hull area is near-constant during moves), 3-fold │
│                          │ symmetric ring/triangle initializations, a targeted push move that directly increases the │
│                          │ selected small triangle's area, and a strict-min polish. I revert the core to that proven │
│                          │ structure while adding the recommended axis-wise fallback moves in the annealer for extra │
│                          │ directional diversity at negligible cost.                                                 │
